@using BlazorRamp.Core.Components.LiveRegion
@attribute [Route(GlobalValues.Path_Frameworks_Modal_Dialog)]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Page_Title_Frameworks_Modal_Dialog</PageTitle>

<h1 class="page-heading">@GlobalValues.Page_Heading_Frameworks_Modal_Dialog</h1>

<p>
    The <span class="code-span">&lt;ModalDialogContainer /&gt;</span> component and the <span class="code-span">ModalDialogService</span> together make up a modal dialog framework that allow you to have your content placed within a an HTML dialog element, with the framework
    taking care of all interactions with the HTML Dialog API.
</p>
<p>
    This content is in the form of a standard blazor component and as such you just define your <span class="code-span">[Parameter]</span> properties as normal as the means of
    passing data into your component. When you want to show your component in a dialog you just invoke the methods on the service providing it with the values for your component parameters.
</p>
<p>
    The framework allows for nested dialogs and as it overrides the default HTML Dialog API escape key mechanism, via a subscription mechanism on the service, it gives you the ability
    to close the dialog when the escape key is pressed, or perhaps open another dialog asking for confirmation if the current dialog was a pop up form with unsaved changes etc.
</p>
<p>
    As the HTML dialog makes everything beneath it inert, this also makes any centralised aria live regions inert. However, the announcement history component and its associated live regions
    react to dialog openings and are moved, so the live regions are always available via the live region service.
</p>


<h2 id="dialog-keyboard" class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Keyboard Interaction</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="dialog-keyboard">
    <li><kbd>Escape</kbd> - closes the top most dialog if a handler has not been specified. If one has been specified then you control whether or not the dialog should close, 
                            as this could be a popup form with unsaved changes, and you may want to get confirmation from the user first etc. 
                            <b>Note: </b> If the Announcement History popover (non-modal dialog) is open, then the escape key will close that first.
        .</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="screen-reader-quirks" class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Screen reader quirks or known issues</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="screen-reader-quirks">
    <li>
        <p>
            <b>Safari browser.</b> Safari has, at the time of writing multiple issues regarding using a popover inside of nested modal dialogs and from my investigations, reordering issues of nested modal dialogs. 
        </p>
            <p>
                What does this mean, there is an edge case that I cannot do anything about. Lets say you open a dialog and then launch another dialog, everything will work perfectly.
                Lets say now in the second nested dialog (it works fine in a single dialog), you open a popover (non-modal dialog) such as the Announcmenet History component. At this point it all works fine, and
                if you use the buttons to close the popover, its OK. But if you use the escape key, the popover will close but then Safari puts the dialogs in the wrong order. You then tab and the ordering is
                restored.
            </p>
            <p>
                <b>Note:</b> All other browsers that I tested workd perfectly without issue, it was only this one edge case when using Safari on macOS (iPhone was not effected given its touch the buttons) I came across when 
                testing. I will from time to time checkup on this, but I cannot control how safari orders the top layer items.
            </p>
    </li>
</ul>

<section>
    <h2 class="page-heading">Installation</h2>

    <p>
        <b>Note: </b> It is a requirement that the Blazor Ramp Core <span class="code-span">core-live-region.js</span> script, <span class="code-span">LiveRegionService</span>, and
        associated <span class="code-span">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> component are added alongside this component’s specific
        requirements (a stylesheet reference in this instance). You do not need to add the BlazorRamp.Core package separately, as BlazorRamp.Core is included as a transitive package, you only need to add the packages that contain the components
        that you wish to use i.e the <b>BlazorRamp.DialogFramework</b> NuGet package.
    </p>
    <p>
        Please follow the instructions in the <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Class" href="@GlobalValues.Path_Overview_Getting_Started">@GlobalValues.Main_Nav_Section_Heading_About - @GlobalValues.Page_Heading_Overview_Getting_Started</a>
        ensuring that in the <span class="code-span">&lt;head&gt;</span> section you also add the Dialog Framework stylesheet as follows:
    </p>
    <CodeBox CodeBlock="@CodeSnippets.Add_Dialog_Framework_Style_Sheet" AriaLabel="Add dialog framew stylesheet code block" />


    <p>
        <b>Next register</b> the dialog framework service in the <span class="code-span">Program.cs</span> file (Both server and client if using Server and WebAssembly interactive rendermode) and
        add the following line to the service registration section if you are using the <span class="code-span">Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection</span> package:
    </p>
    <CodeBox CodeBlock="@CodeSnippets.Add_Dialog_Framework_Service" AriaLabel="Register live region service code block" />
    <p>
        For other Dependency Injection providers, register the type <span class="code-span">ModalDialogService</span> as you normally would.
    </p>

    <p>
        <b>Important</b> Please read carefully so you understand the best place for the <span class="code-span">&lt;ModalDialogContainer /&gt;</span> which acts as the
        root for unlimeited nested dialogs for the specific process.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>Adding</b> the <span class="code-span">&lt;ModalDialogContainer /&gt;</span>
    </p>
    <p>
        The best location really depends on your project type and the following is the recommendation.
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Interactive Server (Global or Per Page)</b> - You can simply place a single instance of the <span class="code-span">&lt;ModalDialogContainer @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" /&gt;</span> in the
                <span class="Routes.razor"></span> file next to the <span class="code-span">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> component.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Interactive WebAssembly (Global or Per Page)</b> - You can simply place a single instance of the <span class="code-span">&lt;ModalDialogContainer @@rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" /&gt;</span> in the
                <span class="Routes.razor"></span> file next to the <span class="code-span">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> component.
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <b>Interactive Auto (Server and WebAssembly) (Global or Per Page)</b>
                </p>
                <p>
                    This is the most complex Blazor scenario, and as such placement is determined by your understanding of the render modes and when you want the downloading of the WebAssembly files to start.
                </p>
                <p>
                    For example you can simply place two instances of the <span>ModalDialogContainer</span> in the <span class="Routes.razor"></span> file next to the
                    <span class="code-span">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> component. One using <span class="code-span">@@rendermode="InteractiveServer"</span> the othere using
                    <span class="code-span">@@rendermode="InteractiveWebAssemlby"</span> and these would handle all Server and WebAssembly page usages.<br />
                    <b>Note: You cannot add another</b> for <span class="code-span">@@rendermode="InteractiveAuto""</span>. For your Auto pages you would place an instance of the
                    <span class="code-span">&lt;ModalDialogContainer @@rendermode="InteractiveAuto" /&gt;</span> on each page that wants to have a dialog / nested dialogs.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Placing an  <span class="code-span">@@rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly"</span> in this project type will cause the WASM files to download immediately as
                    the <span class="code-span">&lt;Router &gt;</span> is run every request.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If this is a little confusing then you can also just simply place an instance of the <span>ModalDialogContainer</span> on each page where you want to have a dialog / nested dialogs
                    and by default it will use the parent components <span class="code-span">@@rendermode</span>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <b>Note:</b> Unlike the <span class="code-span">&lt;AnnouncmentHistory /&gt;</span> that works without an interactive mode, the <span class="code-span">ModalDialogContainer</span>
                    needs one to work correctly.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
        See the next page, <b>Modal Dialog (Using)</b> for examples of how you use the dialog service to open and close dialogs, pass data in, get data out and handle the register a handler for the
        <span class="code-span">Escape</span> key. The dialog framework overrides the default HTML Dialog API so you can choose what you want to do when the <span class="code-span">Escape</span> key
        is pressed.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Dialog service & assoicated classes</h2>
    <p>
        The following provides details of the dialog service and all related classes
    </p>
    <section class="code-table" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">

        <h3 id="dialog-service" class="code-table__heading">ModalDialogService</h3>

        <table aria-labelledby="dialog-service">

            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Returns</th>
                    <th scope="col">Method</th>
                    <th scope="col">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>

                <tr>
                    <td>Task</td>
                    <td>CloseDialog(ModalDialogResult dialogResult)</td>
                    <td>
                        Closes the dialog and sets the opening task as completed, in order for the <span class="code-span">ModalDialogResult</span>  to be returned from the awaited
                        <span class="code-span">ShowModal</span>.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>string</td>
                    <td>GetAriaLabelledByID()</td>
                    <td>
                        Property to get the unique string value that was added to the <span class="code-span">aria-labelledby</span> attribute on the
                        HTML <span class="code-span">dialog</span> elenent. Uss this value for the <span class="code-span">id</span> attribute on your heading to give the dialog an accessible
                        name
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>void</td>
                    <td>RegisterEscapeHandler(Func&lt;Task&gt; handler)</td>
                    <td>
                        Allows for the registering of a handler that will be called when the escape key is pressed for your dialog window.
                        <b>Note: </b>If you do not provide a handler and the escape key is presed the dialog will close and return a cancelled result.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>void</td>
                    <td>UnregisterEscapeHandler(Func&lt;Task&gt; handler)</td>
                    <td>Unregisters the escape handler. <b>Note: </b>Weak references are used but you should call this in your components dispose method.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Task&lt;ModalDialogResult&gt</td>
                    <td>ShowDialog&lt;TDialog&gt;()</td>
                    <td>
                        Shows the dialog containing your component (<span>TDialog</span>) with no paramater data (<sapn class="code-span">ModalDialogParameters&lt;TDialog&gt;</sapn>) and uses the
                        default values for the dialog options, <span class="code-span">ModalDialogOptions</span>.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Task&lt;ModalDialogResult&gt;</td>
                    <td>ShowDialog&lt;TDialog&gt;(ModalDialogOptions dialogOptions)</td>
                    <td>
                        Shows the dialog containing your component (<span>TDialog</span>) with no paramater data, but with <span class="code-span">ModalDialogOptions</span> specified.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Task&lt;ModalDialogResult&gt;</td>
                    <td>ShowDialog&lt;TDialog&gt;(ModalDialogParameters&lt;TDialog&gt; dialogParameters)</td>
                    <td>
                        Shows the dialog containing your component (<span>TDialog</span>) with <span class="code-span">ModalDialogParameters&lt;TDialog&gt;</span> data, using the default values
                        for the dialog otpions, <span class="code-span">ModalDialogOptions</span>.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Task&lt;ModalDialogResult&gt;</td>
                    <td>ShowDialog&lt;TDialog&gt;(ModalDialogParameters&lt;TDialog&gt; dialogParameters, ModalDialogOptions dialogOptions)</td>
                    <td>
                        Shows the dialog containing your component (<span>TDialog</span>) with both <span class="code-span">ModalDialogParameters&lt;TDialog&gt;</span> data
                        andd ialog otpions, <span class="code-span">ModalDialogOptions</span>.
                    </td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

    </section>

    <section class="code-table two-col-descriptive" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">

        <h3 id="dialog-options" class="code-table__heading">ModalDialogOptions class</h3>

        <table aria-labelledby="dialog-options">

            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Type</th>
                    <th scope="col">Property</th>
                    <th scope="col">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>

                <tr>
                    <td>string</td>
                    <td>HorizonalPosition</td>
                    <td>
                        Retruns a value such as <span class="code-span">start</span>, <span class="code-span">center</span> or <span class="code-span">end</span>
                        used for an inline style that sets the horizontal alignment.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>string</td>
                    <td>VerticalPosition</td>
                    <td>
                        Retruns a value such as <span class="code-span">start</span>, <span class="code-span">center</span> or <span class="code-span">end</span>
                        used for an inline style that sets the vertical alignment.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>string</td>
                    <td>MaxWidth</td>
                    <td>
                        Returns a string value with the % sign appended for use in an inline style that sets the maximum width
                        of the dialog window.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td colspan="3">
                        <b>Constructor:</b><br />
                        ModalDialogOptions (HorizontalAlignment horizontalAlignment = HorizontalAlignment.Centre, VerticalAlignment verticalAlignment = VerticalAlignment.Centre, int maxWidthPercent = 70)
                    </td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

    </section>

    <section class="code-table two-col-descriptive" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">

        <h3 id="dialog-parameters" class="code-table__heading">ModalDialogParameter&lt;TDialog&gt; class</h3>

        <table aria-labelledby="dialog-parameters">

            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Returns</th>
                    <th scope="col">Method</th>
                    <th scope="col">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>void</td>
                    <td>Add&lt;TData&gt;(Expression&lt;Func&lt;TDialog, object&gt;&gt; dialogParam, TData? data)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td colspan="3">
                        <b>Parameterless constructor (initialise with the your component type): </b>
                        new ModalDialogParameters&lt;MyComponentType&gt;()
                    </td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

    </section>

    <section class="code-table two-col-descriptive" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">

        <h3 id="dialog-result" class="code-table__heading">ModalDialogResult class</h3>

        <table aria-labelledby="dialog-result">

            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Type/Return</th>
                    <th scope="col">Property/Static Method</th>
                    <th scope="col">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>DialogResultButtons (enum)</td>
                    <td>ButtonClicked</td>
                    <td>Returns the type of button used to close the dilaog.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>object</td>
                    <td>Data</td>
                    <td>Data returned when the dialog is closed.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Type</td>
                    <td>DataType</td>
                    <td>The type of data that was cast as object.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>string</td>
                    <td>ButtonText</td>
                    <td>
                        The text on the button, used for Other when you want more than just OK and Cancel.
                        The <span class="code-span">ButtonText</span> allows you to differentiate between Other buttons.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>ModalDialogResult</td>
                    <td>Cancel&ltT&gt;()</td>
                    <td>Returns a cancelled result with a payload of type <span class="code-span">NoReturnValue</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>ModalDialogResult</td>
                    <td>OK&ltT&gt;()</td>
                    <td>Returns a successful result with a payload of type <span class="code-span">NoReturnValue</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>ModalDialogResult</td>
                    <td>OK&ltT&gt;(T data)</td>
                    <td>Returns a successful result with a payload of type <span class="code-span">T</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>ModalDialogResult</td>
                    <td>Other&ltT&gt;(string buttonText)</td>
                    <td>Returns an Other result with a payload of type <span class="code-span">NoReturnValue</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>ModalDialogResult</td>
                    <td>Other&ltT&gt;(string buttonText, T data)</td>
                    <td>Returns an Other result with a payload of type <span class="code-span">T</span></td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

    </section>

    <section class="code-table two-col-descriptive" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">

        <h3 id="dialog-result" class="code-table__heading">NoReturnValue record (Unit)</h3>

        <table aria-labelledby="dialog-result">

            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Type/Return</th>
                    <th scope="col">Public Field</th>
                    <th scope="col">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>NoReturnValue</td>
                    <td>Value</td>
                    <td>Returns a Unit type of <span class="code-span">NoReturnValue</span> to indicate the return has no meaningful value</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

    </section>

</section>

